  "id": "graph-context-usage",
  "name": "Graph Context Usage Map",
  "tagColors": {
    "agent-cost": "#ef4444",
    "boundary": "#7c3aed",
    "context": "#f59e0b",
    "lightweight": "#22c55e",
    "recommendation": "#14b8a6",
    "risk": "#f97316",
    "storage": "#64748b",
    "tooling": "#0284c7",
    "ui": "#16a34a"
  },
  "nodes": [
    {
      "id": "best-practice",
      "text": "Recommended authoring style\nKeep graph nodes concise, split big maps into named resources, mention only the resources relevant to the current question, and use tags to group meaning rather than long prose.",
      "tags": [
        "lightweight",
        "recommendation"
      ],
      "layout": {
        "position": {
          "x": -260,
          "y": 760
        }
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "bottom-line",
      "text": "Bottom line\nThe system is designed to be agent-light when graphs are concise and explicitly mentioned. It can become heavy if one graph becomes a giant knowledge base or if every chat drags in multiple large resources.",
      "tags": [
        "agent-cost",
        "recommendation"
      ],
      "layout": {
        "position": {
          "x": 260,
          "y": 760
        }
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "cost-drivers",
      "text": "What can make it feel heavy\nLarge resources, many open/mentioned graphs, verbose node text, dense relationship lists, and repeated full context summaries can consume prompt budget and attention.",
      "tags": [
        "agent-cost",
        "risk"
      ],
      "layout": {
        "position": {
          "x": -520,
          "y": 420
        }
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "current-mitigation",
      "text": "Current mitigation model\nResource-oriented mentions + bounded dynamic context + graph tools + media filtering. This keeps graph context closer to a curated brief than a generated codebase dump.",
      "tags": [
        "boundary",
        "context",
        "lightweight"
      ],
      "layout": {
        "position": {
          "x": 0,
          "y": 420
        }
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "live-draft",
      "text": "Live draft is shared state, not automatic prompt bulk\nEditor and AI tools read/write the same live draft. Unsaved graph changes are visible through Graph APIs/context, but they are not automatically serialized as large provider attachments.",
      "tags": [
        "lightweight",
        "storage",
        "tooling"
      ],
      "layout": {
        "position": {
          "x": 615.1036340236398,
          "y": 97.02801143676126
        }
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "no-media",
      "text": "Graph files are filtered from provider media\nManaged graph JSON is omitted from file/media parts in prompt building, V2 runner paths, and legacy V1 conversion. Older data URLs are filtered via FilePart.source.path when needed.",
      "tags": [
        "boundary",
        "lightweight"
      ],
      "layout": {
        "position": {
          "x": 558.6358513221036,
          "y": -254.17786847724554
        }
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "resource-summary",
      "text": "Bounded System Context summary\nThe session context injects a compact textual summary: resource ID, name, revision/digest, tag colors, nodes, and relationships. This gives the agent enough orientation without treating the graph file as raw prompt attachment.",
      "tags": [
        "context",
        "lightweight"
      ],
      "layout": {
        "position": {
          "x": 12.190136439262517,
          "y": -395.3105144758139
        }
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "root",
      "text": "Graph context usage map\nPurpose: explain what our Graph system adds to agent context, where the cost comes from, and why it is intended to stay lightweight.",
      "tags": [
        "agent-cost",
        "context"
      ],
      "layout": {
        "position": {
          "x": -120.92441473647364,
          "y": -79.5380690415139
        }
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "selected-nodes",
      "text": "Selected nodes as task brief\nWhen the user selects graph nodes and asks for work, those nodes become scoped intent. The graph is a brief; the agent still inspects real source files before changing code.",
      "tags": [
        "boundary",
        "context",
        "ui"
      ],
      "layout": {
        "position": {
          "x": 40.11686139309654,
          "y": 156.61949315208605
        }
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "tools-on-demand",
      "text": "Tools are on-demand detail access\nThe agent can list/query/get context and edit nodes/wires through graph_* tools. Detail is pulled only when the task needs it instead of always flooding every provider turn.",
      "tags": [
        "context",
        "tooling"
      ],
      "layout": {
        "position": {
          "x": -520,
          "y": 80
        }
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "user-mention",
      "text": "User mentions a graph resource\nA chat mention like @GraphContextUsageMap identifies the managed resource. The mention is the trigger for scoped graph context, not a request to attach the full JSON file as model media.",
      "tags": [
        "boundary",
        "context",
        "lightweight"
      ],
      "layout": {
        "position": {
          "x": -555.663862758865,
          "y": -294.29971408096856
        }
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "watch-metrics",
      "text": "What to watch while evaluating heaviness\nPrompt token growth per mentioned resource, number of nodes summarized, whether unrelated graphs appear, tool-call count, and whether the agent over-trusts graph notes over source inspection.",
      "tags": [
        "agent-cost",
        "recommendation"
      ],
      "layout": {
        "position": {
          "x": 520,
          "y": 420
        }
      }
    }
  ],
